WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Alright, let's just dive right into this explainer and kick off this next core stage. We're looking at Matt LeMay's brutally honest take on what product management actually looks like on the ground. You know, forget the polished, perfect theories for a second, because today, we're talking about survival.

2
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:25.130
You might feel like product management is supposed to be this neat, linear process, right? Just brilliant strategies, perfectly aligned teams, and crystal clear, data-driven decisions.

3
00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:35.250
But as you've probably kind of started to sense by now, the reality is, well, it's usually sideways meetings, sudden executive pivots, missing data, and unpredictable office politics.

4
00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:39.530
So let's get into part one, the messy product reality.

5
00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:47.380
We're going to start this stage by totally stripping away the syllabus theory and shattering probably the biggest illusion in the whole industry.

6
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:52.220
And that is the idea that a product manager is the mini-CEO of the product.

7
00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:53.360
Let's be real.

8
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.860
You aren't a mini-CEO barking orders from some corner office.

9
00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:01.140
The truth is, you have enormous responsibility for the product's success,

10
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.680
but almost zero formal authority over the people actually building it.

11
00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:11.580
You're really a leader who has to earn influence and trust literally every single day just to get things done.

12
00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.050
Becoming the connective tissue.

13
00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:15.940
So think about it.

14
00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:21.420
If you aren't the undisputed brain of the operation barking out orders, what exactly are you?

15
00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:27.060
Well, LeMay argues that your real job actually lives in the white space on the organizational chart.

16
00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.960
You are the connective tissue, linking engineering, leadership, and the end users.

17
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:35.400
You're the one making sure developers truly get the business needs,

18
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.300
designers understand the technical constraints, and leadership actually sees the real progress.

19
00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:44.620
Basically, you're the ultimate translator between all these totally different departments.

20
00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:46.660
Now, here's the kicker.

21
00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:50.960
When connective tissue does its job perfectly, it's completely invisible.

22
00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:56.760
You are definitely not getting a standing ovation for simply preventing a misunderstanding between design and engineering.

23
00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:00.600
Nobody's writing, hey, there were no misunderstandings this week on your performance review.

24
00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:07.300
But that invisible behind-the-scenes coordination, that is exactly what makes the product work and actually gets it out the door.

25
00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:09.960
Mastering the core skills.

26
00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:18.460
To be that highly effective connective tissue, you really don't need a fancy computer science degree or some magical mastery of the latest frameworks.

27
00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:24.380
What you actually need are four surprisingly boring but incredibly powerful foundational skills.

28
00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.540
We call this the CORE acronym.

29
00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:30.140
Communicate, Organize, Research, Execute.

30
00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.480
And notice how these are all active verbs.

31
00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.360
You have to practice them daily if you want to survive the chaos.

32
00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:41.960
You have to actively communicate clear messages, organize processes that actually help your team instead of burdening them,

33
00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:47.700
research by living in your user's actual reality, and execute by constantly removing blockers.

34
00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:51.430
Let's look at Michael from Velox, our running example for this course.

35
00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:57.350
She deployed the absolute ultimate communication tool just by asking this seemingly obvious,

36
00:02:57.550 --> 00:02:59.590
almost embarrassing question during a feature kickoff.

37
00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:05.190
She literally just stopped and asked, wait, what kind of notification are we talking about here?

38
00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:07.110
Email, mobile, or in-app?

39
00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:10.000
It sounds so incredibly simple, right?

40
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.620
But by choosing clarity over the comfort of just nodding along and staying quiet,

41
00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:18.060
Michael's 10-second question revealed a massive misalignment.

42
00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:20.520
Literally everyone in the room had a different assumption.

43
00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:22.920
Developers were planning a mobile push notification,

44
00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:25.000
the designer was mocking up an in-app banner,

45
00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:27.840
and sales had already promised the customer a daily email.

46
00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.140
That one brave question saved three weeks of conflicting development work.

47
00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:36.400
And that, right there, is what effective connective tissue looks like in practice.

48
00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:40.250
All right, part four, navigating the gray areas.

49
00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:44.950
All of this builds up to how you make decisions when you just don't have all the answers.

50
00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:48.610
Because real product management, it's all about living in the gray.

51
00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:53.410
You are always going to face partial data, conflicting opinions, and taking clocks.

52
00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:58.790
The average PM usually tries to clear all the fog by adding more committees and doing more research.

53
00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:02.630
But the excellent PM learns to make decisions in that fog.

54
00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:08.950
Actually, if you wait for a perfectly clear picture, complete certainty, your opportunity is going to be long gone.

55
00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:13.460
Here is exactly how Michal handled a classic executive demand.

56
00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:17.720
The CEO rushed in, urgently demanding an AI chatbot within a month.

57
00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:27.780
Now, instead of panicking or just outright refusing, she proposed a really cheap one-week prototype test with five users to see what specific problem it would actually solve.

58
00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:31.340
And guess what? That quick test completely cleared the fog.

59
00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.140
The data revealed users didn't even want a chat interface.

60
00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:36.380
They just wanted automated task summaries.

61
00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:41.900
Boom. She delivered real value and saved a quarter of unnecessary development time.

62
00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:46.980
Navigating organizational politics like this, you know, it isn't a dirty word.

63
00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:53.720
It's simply how organizations make decisions, and you absolutely have to work within that reality rather than just fighting it blindly.

64
00:04:54.620 --> 00:05:01.940
Meekly saying yes to every single executive demand or defiantly screaming no, both of those are absolute failures to do the job.

65
00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:07.360
The real work is asking about the goal, proposing a test, and bringing back solid evidence.

66
00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:13.520
So before you dive into the reading for this stage of the course, I want you to keep these actionable principles in mind.

67
00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:19.900
First, start with the actual work and the problem, not by trying to force some fancy new framework onto your team.

68
00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:24.560
Second, always ask why before sitting through another pointless meeting.

69
00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:28.700
Third, actively choose clarity, even when it's super uncomfortable.

70
00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:33.320
And please, please cut your long email drafts in half so people actually read them.

71
00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.720
These are the real, practical tools that are going to stop you from breaking against chaotic reality.

72
00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:41.370
Now it's completely your turn.

73
00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:48.490
Jump right into this stage's reading materials, discover the specifics of how to really thrive in the mess, and start asking yourself right now.

74
00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:52.110
How are you going to connect your teams and clear the fog today?

75
00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:54.970
Have fun with the material, and I'll see you in the next stage.

